<div id="outline-container-orgbed10ef" class="outline-3">
<h3 id="orgbed10ef"><span class="section-number-3">4.4</span> Weather info</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-4-4">
<p>
I like sailing and weather conditions are something to look at,
I've tested several programs on terminal that I found useful, here are.
</p>
</div>

<div id="outline-container-orgab1ce9f" class="outline-4">
<h4 id="orgab1ce9f"><span class="section-number-4">4.4.1</span> Using inxi</h4>
<div class="outline-text-4" id="text-4-4-1">
<p>
Inxi is a Command line system information for console and IRC,
this allows you to have many information about the system and also
Weather information, visit it's manual page. 
</p>

<p>
This is not an standard tool so you will need to install it using
your package manager.
</p>


<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-sh">
inxi -W Alicante,Spain

</pre>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div id="outline-container-orgd394383" class="outline-4">
<h4 id="orgd394383"><span class="section-number-4">4.4.2</span> Using wttr.in</h4>
<div class="outline-text-4" id="text-4-4-2">
<p>
Having <b>curl</b> installed in your system this is a good tool with
propper information and lightweight due the fact that is not needed
any installation.
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-sh">
curl wttr.in/LEAL?lang=es 

</pre>
</div>

<p>
It's complete information but at the same time is way too much if
you just want the minimum information.
</p>
</div>
</div>

<div id="outline-container-org4ad9818" class="outline-4">
<h4 id="org4ad9818"><span class="section-number-4">4.4.3</span> Using OpenWeatherMap API</h4>
<div class="outline-text-4" id="text-4-4-3">
<p>
One solution is made it yourself by using the OpenWeatherMap API,
you can register (I did, no confirmation link sent me back&#x2026;)
</p>

<p>
Once I will get the confirmation link maybe I could use this:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-sh">
curl api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather?q=Alicante&amp;appid=

</pre>
</div>
</div>
</div>


<div id="outline-container-org5de1caa" class="outline-4">
<h4 id="org5de1caa"><span class="section-number-4">4.4.4</span> Using weather-util</h4>
<div class="outline-text-4" id="text-4-4-4">
<p>
Nice utility from one-developer, nice to read the README of this
tool, the "Why" section is heart-warmming. Love is the answer.
</p>

<p>
You should visit and download the latest tar at 
&gt; <a href="http://fungi.yuggoth.org/weather/src/">http://fungi.yuggoth.org/weather/src/</a>
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-sh">
weather -a LEAL 

</pre>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div id="outline-container-orgc1b05ab" class="outline-4">
<h4 id="orgc1b05ab"><span class="section-number-4">4.4.5</span> Using Puertos del Estado</h4>
<div class="outline-text-4" id="text-4-4-5">
<p>
For maritime forecast in Spain I use Puertos del Estado web page
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-sh">
curl -X POST -d 'point=2079100&amp;name=Alicante' https://bancodatos.puertos.es/TablaAccesoSimplificado/util/get_wanadata.php | html2text

</pre>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div id="outline-container-org3cb440f" class="outline-3">
<h3 id="org3cb440f"><span class="section-number-3">4.5</span> Fonts</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-4-5">
<p>
Source Code Pro is the default font for Emacs, and also is a nice font. Here is how to install it.
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-sh">
git clone https://github.com/adobe-fonts/source-code-pro.git ./source-code-pro
mkdir ~/.fonts
cp ./source-code-pro/OTF/*.otf ~/.fonts/
fc-cache -f -v

</pre>
</div>
